; The TUPLE path type-checks its components against the field types.
;
; A construction that matches no init# is filled POSITIONALLY, and until
; report_component_type_mismatch# landed nothing compared component i against
; property i: only the COUNT was ever checked -- too many arguments is `wrong
; number of arguments`, too few without defaults is fill_default_components# --
; and everything between them was accepted at rc 0 and RAN.
;
; This is the gate the Slice layout question turned on. `Slice[T]` declares
; only `init ()`, so no init of arity 2 could ever match `Slice[T](p, n)` and
; the exact-arity branch of fill_default_components# deliberately says nothing
; there; flipping that field list would have silently reversed every one of
; the tree's 306 constructions. Measured with the gate live: the same flip is
; 2839 diagnostics over the six package roots, both directions at every site.
;
; The pointee here is `char` ON PURPOSE. type_conformance_accepted#'s class 8
; strips one level of indirection off the source and re-asks, so with it live
; `pointer[char]` filling a `size_t` field is accepted (char is an integer)
; while `pointer[Leaf]` in the same slot is caught -- a gate that answers by
; the POINTEE's family is not a gate.
;
; xfail: cannot fill Hold.n

define Hold (n: size_t, p: pointer[char])

function build(v: pointer[char], len: size_t) returns int
{
    ; SWAPPED: the buffer into the length field, the length into the pointer.
    let h Hold(v, len)
    1
}

print "FAIL"
exit 0
